Put the automatic-cleanup settings behind one row instead of five on the page.

Privacy and security had grown three Aerium blocks stacked inline above
"Delete browsing data": the delete-on-exit toggle with its eight data types and
its never-delete list, the delete-when-the-last-tab-closes toggle with its
delay field and its cache option, and the per-site rules table with its Add
button. Between the page title and the first upstream row there were five
switches, a number input and a table. Each control is worth having; all of them
at once, before you have decided you want any of them, is not a privacy page.

So they move behind a single row, "Automatic cleanup", which opens a subpage
holding the same three elements in the same order. Nothing is removed and no
control changes behaviour - this is regrouping. Someone who wants the defaults
now reads one line instead of scrolling past a form; someone who wants to
configure it goes in and configures all of it in one place, which is where it
belonged.

Mechanics

  The subpage is a cr-view-manager view like every other Privacy subpage:
  registered in privacy_page_index.html with data-parent-view-id="privacy",
  routed by AERIUM_CLEANUP (declared in router.ts, created in route.ts as a
  child of PRIVACY at /automaticCleanup), and imported in lazy_load.ts, which
  is where the sibling subpage elements are imported rather than in
  privacy_page_index.ts.

  The element composes SettingsViewMixin for the routePath every view forwards
  to its settings-subpage, and PrefsMixin because it passes prefs through to
  all three children. It holds no controls of its own - the three elements are
  untouched, still own their prefs, and still have exactly one host each.

  privacy_page.ts loses the three imports (they move to the new page), and
  gains the click handler, the focus-config entry that returns focus to the row
  on back-navigation, and the getAssociatedControlFor case that ties the view
  id to the row.

Two details that are easy to get wrong here

  Divider bookkeeping. Rows in this section draw their separator as a top
  border via class="hr", and the convention is that the first row omits it. The
  Aerium block was first and carried no "hr"; the new row takes that position
  and likewise carries none, so clearBrowsingData keeps the "hr" it was given
  when the block moved above it. The visible structure of the page is
  unchanged.

  The icon is cr:delete, the same as the row below it. That is deliberate
  rather than an oversight: the two rows are the automatic and the one-shot
  version of the same act, and they read as a pair. It is also an icon known
  to resolve - cr-icon draws nothing at all for an iconset:id pair that does
  not, which is a silent failure this page has been bitten by before.


